# sent_id = 1.20.2
# text = est tibi non infra speciem, non nomine dispar, Theiodamanteo proximus ardor Hylae: hunc tu, sive leges umbrosae flumina silvae, sive Aniena tuos tinxerit unda pedes, sive Gigantea spatiabere litoris ora, sive ubicumque vago fluminis hospitio, Nympharum semper cupidas defende rapinas (non minor Ausoniis est amor adriacis); ne sint duri montes et frigida saxa, Galle, neque expertos semper adire lacus: quae miser ignotis error perpessus in oris Herculis indomito fleverat Ascanio.
# prose_translation = You have a passion not inferior in appearance, not unequal in name, close to Theiodamas' Hylas: defend him, whether you read the streams of the shady forest, or the Anio's wave has bathed your feet, or you stroll along the Giant's shore, or wherever you wander with a river's hospitality, always against the greedy seizures of the Nymphs (nor is love less for the Ausonian Adriatic); let not the mountains be harsh and the rocks cold, Gallus, nor always approach the pools you have tried: which wretched error, having suffered in unknown lands, had wept for Hercules' untamed Ascanius.
# literal_translation = is to-you not below appearance, not in-name unequal, Theiodamantean nearest ardor of-Hylas: him you, whether you-read of-shady forest streams, whether Anienian wave your feet has-wet, whether Gigantean you-will-walk of-shore edge, whether wherever with-wandering of-river hospitality, of-Nymphs always greedy defend seizures (not less for-Ausonian is love for-Adriatic); let-not be harsh mountains and cold rocks, Gallus, nor tried always to-approach lakes: which wretched in-unknown error having-suffered in-lands had-wept of-Hercules with-untamed Ascanius.
