# sent_id = 2.1.15
# text = quandocumque igitur vitam mea fata reposcent, et breve in exiguo marmore nomen ero, Maecenas, nostrae spes invidiosa iuventae, et vitae et morti gloria iusta meae, si te forte meo ducet via proxima busto, esseda caelatis siste Britanna iugis, taliaque illacrimans mutae iace verba favillae : "Huic misero fatum dura puella fuit."
# prose_translation = Whenever, therefore, my fates shall demand my life back, and I shall be a brief name on a small marble slab, Maecenas, the envied hope of our youth, and the just glory of both my life and my death, if by chance the nearest road shall lead you past my tomb, stop your British chariot with its engraved harness, and weeping, cast these words to the silent ashes: "For this wretched man, a hard girl was his fate."
# literal_translation = Whenever therefore life my fates shall-demand, and brief on small marble a-name I-shall-be, Maecenas, of-our hope envied of-youth, and of-life and of-death glory just of-mine, if you by-chance by-my shall-lead road nearest to-tomb, chariot with-engraved stop British with-harness, and-such weeping to-silent cast words to-ashes: "To-this wretched fate hard girl was."
