# sent_id = 2.23.7
# text = cui saepe immundo Sacra conteritur Via socco, nec sinit esse moram, si quis adire velit; differet haec numquam, nec poscet garrula, quod te astrictus ploret saepe dedisse pater, nec dicet "Timeo, propera iam surgere, quaeso : infelix, hodie vir mihi rure venit." et quas Euphrates et quas mihi misit Orontes, me iuerint: nolim furta pudica tori; libertas quoniam nulli iam restat amanti : nullus . . . si quis amare volet.
# prose_translation = For whom the Sacred Way is often worn down by a filthy shoe, and she does not allow any delay, if anyone should wish to approach; she will never put this off, nor will she, chattering, demand that a father, bound by oath, often weep that he has given you away, nor will she say: "I'm afraid, hurry now to get up, I beg you: unlucky me, today my husband comes from the country." And whatever the Euphrates and whatever the Orontes sent me, may they help me: I would not want the chaste thefts of a bed; since freedom now remains for no lover: none . . . if anyone should wish to love.
# literal_translation = for whom often with filthy the Sacred Way is worn down shoe, nor she allows to be delay, if anyone to approach should wish; she will put off this never, nor will demand chattering, that you bound by oath often weep to have given father, nor will say "I fear, hurry now to rise, I beg: unlucky, today husband to me from country comes." and what Euphrates and what to me sent Orontes, me may help: I would not wish chaste thefts of bed; freedom since to no one now remains to lover: none . . . if anyone to love should wish.
