# sent_id = 4.1.20
# text = tunc animi venere Deci Brutique secures, vexit et ipsa sui Caesaris arma Venus, arma resurgentis portans victricia Troiae : felix terra tuos cepit, Iule, deos, si modo Avernalis tremulae cortina Sibyllae dixit Aventino rura pianda Remo, aut si Pergameae sero rata carmina vatis longaevum ad Priami vera fuere caput: "Vertite equum, Danai! male vincitis! Ilia tellus vivet, et huic cineri Iuppiter arma dabit!" optima nutricum nostris lupa Martia rebus, qualia creverunt moenia lacte tuo!
# prose_translation = Then came the spirits of Decius and the axes of Brutus, and Venus herself carried the arms of her own Caesar, bearing the victorious arms of rising Troy: happy land, Iulus, that received your gods, if only the tripod of the trembling Sibyl of Avernus said that the fields were to be purified for Aventine Remus, or if the prophecies of the Pergamean seer, though late, were true for the long-lived head of Priam: "Turn the horse, Greeks! You conquer badly! The Trojan land will live, and Jupiter will give arms to this ash!" Best of nurses, Mars' she-wolf, for our affairs, such walls grew from your milk!
# literal_translation = Then came the spirits of Decius and of Brutus the axes, carried and herself of her own Caesar arms Venus, arms of rising bearing victorious of Troy : happy land your gods received, Iulus, if only of Avernus trembling tripod of Sibyl said on Aventine fields to be purified for Remus, or if of Pergamean late ratified prophecies of seer for long-lived of Priam true were head: "Turn the horse, Greeks! badly you conquer! Trojan land will live, and to this ash Jupiter arms will give!" Best of nurses our affairs she-wolf of Mars, such grew walls from milk your!
