# sent_id = 4.3.13
# text = noctibus hibernis castrensia pensa laboro et Tyria in gladios vellera secta suo; et disco, qua parte fluat vincendus Araxes, quot sine aqua Parthus milia currat equus; cogor et e tabula pictos ediscere mundos, qualis et haec docti sit positura dei, quae tellus sit lenta gelu, quae putris ab aestu, ventus in Italiam qui bene vela ferat.
# prose_translation = On winter nights I work at the camp's wool-spinning tasks, and Tyrian fleeces cut into swords by my own hand; and I learn in what region the Araxes, destined to be conquered, flows, how many miles the Parthian horse runs without water; I am compelled also to learn from a painted map the world's regions, and what the position of this learned god may be, which land is stiff with frost, which crumbles from heat, and what wind may carry our sails well to Italy.
# literal_translation = On-nights winter camp-tasks I-work and Tyrian into swords fleeces cut by-my-own; and I-learn, in-what part flow being-conquered Araxes, how-many without water Parthian miles runs horse; I-am-compelled also and from tablet painted to-learn worlds, what also this of-learned god may-be position, which land is stiff with-frost, which crumbly from heat, wind into Italy which well sails may-carry.
